To calculate the Gibbs free energy change (ΔG°) for the electrochemical reaction, we can use the Nernst equation:

ΔG° = -nFE°

where n is the number of moles of electrons transferred, F is the Faraday constant (96,485 C/mol), and E° is the standard cell potential.

First, we need to find the standard cell potential (E°cell) for the given reaction. We can do this by adding the standard reduction potential of the reaction that occurs as a reduction to the standard reduction potential of the reaction that occurs as an oxidation.

In this reaction, Cu2+ is reduced to Cu (Cu2+ + 2e- → Cu) and Zn is oxidized to Zn2+ (Zn → Zn2+ + 2e-).

E°(cell) = E°(reduction) - E°(oxidation)

E°(reduction) = E°(Cu2+|Cu) = +0.74 V
E°(oxidation) = -E°(Zn2+|Zn) = -(-0.76 V) = +0.76 V

E°(cell) = +0.34 V - (-0.76 V) = +1.10 V

Now we can calculate the Gibbs free energy change:

ΔG° = -nFE°
ΔG° = -(2 mol)(96,485 C/mol)(+1.10 V)

Remember that 1 V = 1 J/C, so the units are consistent.

ΔG° = -200,000 J/mol

The correct value for the standard reduction potential of Cu2+/Cu is +0.34 V. Recomputing the cell potential and ΔG°:

E°( reduction) = E°(Cu2+|Cu) = +0.34 V
E°(oxidation) = -E°(Zn2+|Zn) = -(-0.76 V) = +0.76 V

E°(cell) = E°(reduction) - E°(oxidation) = +0.34 V - (-0.76 V) = +1.10 V

ΔG° = -nFE°
ΔG° = -(2 mol)(96,485 C/mol)(+1.10 V)
ΔG° = -200000 J/mol

