","When the complex `[Pt(NH3)4Cl2]Cl2` dissolves in water, it dissociates into its constituent ions. The formula of the complex is `[Pt(NH3)4Cl2]Cl2`. This means the central platinum (Pt) ion is bonded to four ammonia (NH3) ligands and two chloride (Cl) ligands, and there are two additional chloride ions outside the complex.

When dissolved in water, the complex dissociates as follows:

`[Pt(NH3)4Cl2]Cl2 → [Pt(NH3)4Cl2]²⁺ + 2Cl⁻`

The `[Pt(NH3)4Cl2]²⁺` ion remains as a complex ion, but the two chloride ions (Cl⁻) are released into the solution as free chloride ions. Therefore, the number of chloride ions produced is 2.",fluency,"# Task Description
