","To prove that condition (i) implies condition (ii), we follow the following steps:  
1. Let \( C \) be a closed set.  
2. For each \( n \), define the function \( \chi_C \) as:  
   \[
   \chi_C(x) =
   \begin{cases}
   1 & \text{if } x \in C \\
   0 & \text{if } x \notin C
   \end{cases}
   \]  
3. Since \( C \) is a closed set, it is bounded. Moreover, \( \chi_C \) is bounded and the sum of the trivial functions \( \chi_C \) and \( -\chi_C \) is zero, so \( \chi_C \) is a function in \( L^1(C) \). Thus, \( \chi_C \) is bounded and continuous.  
4. By condition (i), \( \lim_n \int \chi_C \, d\mu_n = \int \chi_C \, d\mu \).  

5. For any set \( A \), the integral of the trivial function over \( A \) is \( \int \chi_A \, d\mu = \mu(A) \). Thus, \( \int \chi_C \, d\mu = \mu(C) \).  
