<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the work done, we use the formula:

\[
W = F \cdot d \cdot \cos(\theta)
\]

Where:
- \( W \) is the work done
- \( F \) is the applied force
- \( d \) is the distance over which the force is applied
- \( \theta \) is the angle between the force vector and the displacement vector

Given:
- \( F = 60.0 \, \text{N} \)
- \( \theta = 60.0^\circ \)
- \( d = 10.0 \, \text{m} \)

Substituting the values:
\[
W = 60.0 \, \text{N} \cdot 10.0 \, \text{m} \cdot \cos(60.0^\circ)
\]

Since \( \cos(60.0^\circ) = 0.5 \):
\[
W = 600 \, \text{N} \cdot \text{m} \cdot 0.5 = 300 \, \text{J}
\]

Thus, the work done is \(\boxed{300 \, \text{J}}\).
