<USER_REQUEST>
What is the magnetic field strength at the center of a circular coil with 100 turns that carries a current of 1 A and has a radius of 0.2 m?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The magnetic field strength (B) at the center of a circular wire coil is given by the formula:

\[
B = \frac{\mu_0 N I}{2r}
\]

where:
- \( B \) is the magnetic field strength (in Tesla)
- \( \mu_0 \) is the permeability of free space (\( 4.0 \times 10^{-7} \, \text{T} \cdot \text{m} \text{/A} \))
- \( N \) is the number of turns in the loop
- \( I \) is the current (in Amperes)
- \( r \) is the radius of the loop (in meters)

Substituting the given values:
- \( N = 100 \)
- \( I = 1 \, \text{A} \)
- \( r = 0.2 \, \text{m} \)

\[
B = \frac{(4.0 \times 10^{-7} \, \text{T} \cdot \text{m/A}) \times 100 \times 1 \, \text{A}}{2 \times 0.2 \, \text{m}}
\]

\[
B = \frac{4.0 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{T} \cdot \text{m}}{0.4 \, \text{m}}
\]

\[
B = 1.0 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{T}
\]

The magnetic field strength at the center of the coil is \(\boxed{1.0 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{T}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

