","The coefficient of restitution \( e \) is given by:
\[
e = \frac{v_2}{v_1}
\]
where \( v_1 \) is the velocity of the block along the normal to the wall (velocity of approach) and \( v_2 \) is the velocity of the block after the collision (velocity of separation) along the normal to the body.

Given \( e = \frac{1}{3} \), we have:
\[
v_2 = e v_1 = \frac{1}{3} v_1
\]
The velocity of the ball along the normal to the wall is \( v_1 \). After the collision, the velocity of the ball along the normal to the wall is \( v_2 = \frac{1}{3} v_1 \).

The velocity component perpendicular to the normal is unchanged during the collision, as the block is moving horizontally and the block is vertical. Thus, the component of velocity along the horizontal direction remains the same.

The velocity of the ball after the collision is:
\[
\vec{v} = v_x \hat{i} + v_y \hat{j}
\]
The velocity of the ball before the collision is:
\[
\vec{v} = v_x \hat{i}
\]
The velocity component perpendicular to the wall is \( v_1 \). The velocity component parallel to the axis is \( v_x \). The velocity after collision is:
\[
v_2 = \frac{1}{3} v_1
\]
The velocity component perpendicular to the wall after collision is \( \frac{1}{3} v_1 \). The velocity component parallel to the axis remains the same, \( v_x \).

The total velocity of the ball after the collision is:
\[
