I(\lambda, T) = \frac{10^2}{10^{-5} \theta^4} \frac{2 h c^2}{\lambda^5} \left( \frac{1}{e^{\frac{hc}{\lambda k_B T}}} - 1 \right)
\]

where:
- \( I(\lambda, T) \) is the spectral intensity (power per unit area, per unit solid angle, per unit wavelength) in W m⁻² sr⁻¹ m⁻¹
- \( \lambda \) is the wavelength of the radiation in meters
- \( T \) is the temperature of the body in Kelvin
- \( h \) is Planck's constant, \( \boxed{6.626 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}} \)
- \( c \) is the speed of light in vacuum, \( \boxed{3.00 \times 10^8 \text{ m/s}} \)
- \( k_B \) is the Boltzmann constant, \( \boxed{1.381 \times 10^{-23} \text{ J/K}} \)
- \( \theta = \frac{1000}{T} \)

This formula is derived from Planck's's radiation law.

