\[
\Delta E \Delta t \approx \hbar
\]

where \(\hbar = \frac{h}{2\pi} \approx 1.054 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}\) is the reduced Planck constant.

First, we need to convert the given decay time from seconds to Joules:

\[
\Delta t = 2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}
\]

Now, we can find the uncertainty in energy:

\[
\Delta E \approx \frac{\hbar}{\Delta t} = \frac{1.054 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}}{2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}} = 5.27 \times 10^{-12} \text{ J}
\]

The given mass is 1385 MeV. To convert this to Joules, we use the conversion factor \(1 \text{ MeV} = 1.602 \times 10^{-13} \text{ J}\):

\[
m = 1385 \text{ MeV} \times 1.602 \times 10^{-13} \text{ J/MeV} = 2.221 \times 10^{-10} \text{ J}
\]

The uncertainty in energy is \(5.27 \times 10^{-12} \text{ J}\). To find the percentage uncertainty, we divide \(\Delta E\) by the mass \(m\):

\[
\text{Percentage uncertainty} = \frac{\Delta E}{m} \times 100 = \frac{5.27 \times 10^{-12} \text{ J}}{2.221 \times 10^{-10} \text{ J}} \times 100 \approx 0.238\%
\]

This means the measured mass will vary within +/- 0.238% of the known value.

